Evaluation distributes over the powerdomain of a function space.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`papplyEval`).** Define powerdomain evaluation by requiring every function neighbourhood in the first family and every argument neighbourhood in the second to evaluate into some member of the result family.

**Theorem (`papplyEval_rel`).** Powerdomain evaluation relates three families exactly when they are valid powerdomain neighbourhoods and every function–argument pair has an evaluation witness in the result family.

*Proof.* This is precisely the relation defining powerdomain evaluation. ∎

**Definition (`papplyB`).** Define uncurried powerdomain application by viewing powerdomain evaluation as a map out of the product of function and argument powerdomains.

**Definition (`papply`).** Define powerdomain application as the currying of uncurried powerdomain evaluation.

**Theorem (`papplyEval_rel_Ypd_iff`).** On canonical presentations, powerdomain evaluation holds exactly when every decoded function index and decoded argument index have a decoded result index witnessing ordinary evaluation.

*Proof.* Forward, apply powerdomain evaluation to the basic function and argument neighbourhoods selected by the decoded indices, then choose a basic result neighbourhood below the witness. Reverse, choose decoded basic neighbourhoods below arbitrary function and argument members and enlarge the resulting basic evaluation witness by monotonicity. ∎

**Theorem (`papplyEval_step_witness`).** A principal family generated by a single step function evaluates its principal argument family into the corresponding principal result family.

*Proof.* Every function in the principal family contains the generating step, and every argument in the principal argument family contains its input neighbourhood. The step relation therefore sends that argument to the generating result neighbourhood, which itself belongs to the principal result family. ∎

**Theorem (`re_forallG_forallX_existsY`).** Recursive enumerability is preserved by bounded universal quantification over two decoded finite lists followed by bounded existential quantification over a third.

*Proof.* First combine the enumerable relation with decidable membership in the result list and project out the result witness. Then apply bounded universal quantification over the argument list and over the function list, rearranging the nested pair coding between these stages. ∎

**Theorem (`papplyEval_isComputable`).** If ordinary evaluation on the chosen presentations is recursively enumerable, then powerdomain evaluation on their canonical finite-family presentations is recursively enumerable.

*Proof.* The canonical-code characterization identifies powerdomain evaluation with two bounded universal quantifiers and one bounded existential quantifier applied to ordinary evaluation. The preceding closure theorem therefore makes this coded relation recursively enumerable. ∎
